# The conversions of the prelude the elaborator puts in where a type differs from the one wanted (elaborate/convert.wap),
# beside the one of data.wap from a subset to its parent: an explicit program read after data.wap by the theory's
# door, each an overload of `convert`.

# a bool where a proposition is wanted is the proposition that it is yes: `where #it == n`, nat's test of equality, is
# a condition (notes/elaborate.md "Operators, traits and conditions (E3)", `==` at a type with its own test)
convert(b: bool): Prop := Equal[1](bool, b, bool.yes)
# a whole number where an integer is wanted is that integer: `2 * x` for an int x multiplies two ints
convert(n: nat): int := int.of_nat(n)
